          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> Let’s first start by looking at the origins of open source code.</p>
<p>The idea of making source code open and freely available, originated in 1983 with an ideological movement. Richard Stallman a programmer from MIT, was the informal founder, who believed that software should be accessible to programmers to modify as they wished to hopefully improve it. He released free code under his own license under the GNU public license.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> This ideology and new approach eventually led to the Open Source Initiative [OSI] in 1998. The OSI acts as an informational and governing repository of Open source software providing rules and guidelines on how to use it, as well as providing code licensing information and support.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Open source code is usually stored in a public repository and shared with the public. Anyone can access it and use the code independently or contribute to the design or functionality of the project.</p>
<p>Programmers who have access to it can change a program by adding to it or fixing parts that aren't working properly.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Open source software [OSS] usually comes with a distribution license including terms of how developers can use, study, modify and distribute the software. When the source code is changed the OSS must include what was altered as well as the methods used to alter it.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Some well known examples of OSS are:</p>
<p>GNU/Linux-operating system</p>
<p>Mozilla Firefox- web browser</p>
<p>GIMP- photo editing suite</p>
<p>Open Office- office suite with word processing, spreadsheets, and databases</p>
<p>Advantages with OSS are that it is free and comes with a large community of users and programmers that continuously modify the software. This source code is publicly available and also opens up opportunities for new programmers.</p>
<p>The disadvantages are it can have compatibility issues and by it's nature it has problems with quality, performance and security flaws, but the large groups of programming and coding contributors identify and fix these issues.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Let’s now have a look at Closed source code.</p>
<p>Closed software [proprietary] containing closed source code is owned by commercial organisations with creators having copyright over whatever they create. But source code can be easy to copy and take apart to see how it works. The solution to this is by not publishing the source code but instead to compile it into a set of machine code instructions in an executable file and sell that to customers. This is closed source software as it is difficult for anyone to work out what the original source code was by just referring to the executable file.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Commercial organisations invest a great deal of money on developing their software and they want to make it profitable therefore making it closed source is a better option. They will also make it more sophisticated to attract people to buy it rather than download open source equivalents which don't have the same sophistication.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Some well known examples of closed source programs are:</p>
<p>Microsoft Windows – the Windows operating system</p>
<p>Apple macOS – the Apple operating system</p>
<p>Adobe Photoshop – Adobe’s closed source photo-editing suite</p>
<p>Microsoft office – Microsoft’s word processing, spreadsheet, and database software</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
